<div>
<h2 id="instapaper-epub-intro">Read Your Saved Articles Anywhere with EPUB</h2>

<p><a href="https://www.instapaper.com" rel="nofollow">Instapaper</a> has long been the go-to read-later service for saving web articles, blog posts, and long-form content. With the release of <strong>Instapaper 10</strong> in late July 2026 — the biggest update to the iOS app in over a decade — the platform has never been more polished. New features include a Liquid Glass design overhaul, Home Screen widgets, four new reading fonts, and a fully rebuilt web platform.</p>

<p>But here's the real question for e-reader owners: <strong>how do you get those saved articles onto your Kindle, Kobo, or PocketBook?</strong> The answer is EPUB export. Instapaper has offered EPUB exports for years, and with the Instapaper 10 refresh, the workflow is smoother than ever.</p>

<p>This guide covers every method for converting your Instapaper library to EPUB — from Instapaper's own export tools to Calibre automation, browser extensions, and batch workflows. Whether you own a Kindle Paperwhite, a Kobo Libra Colour, or a PocketBook Era Lite, these techniques will put your saved reading on any e-reader screen.</p>

<h2 id="instapaper-native-export">Method 1: Instapaper's Native EPUB Export</h2>

<p>The simplest way to get your saved articles as EPUB files is Instapaper's own export feature. Every article you save in Instapaper can be downloaded as a single EPUB file, perfect for side-loading onto an e-reader.</p>

<h3>Exporting a Single Article</h3>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Open your Instapaper folder</strong> — browse your saved articles on the web or in the iOS app.</li>
  <li><strong>Click the gear icon</strong> (web) or the "..." menu (app) next to any article.</li>
  <li><strong>Select "Download EPUB"</strong> — Instapaper generates a cleanly formatted EPUB of that article with the article's title as the filename.</li>
  <li><strong>Transfer to your e-reader</strong> — connect your Kindle via USB and drag the file into the "Documents" folder, or use Kobo's built-in browser to download it directly.</li>
</ol>

<p>The exported EPUB includes Instapaper's excellent article formatting: clean typography, embedded images, preserved hyperlinks, and metadata like author and publication date. Instapaper's article extraction is among the best in the industry, stripping away ads, sidebars, and clutter before you even export.</p>

<h3>Exporting Multiple Articles (Folder or Highlights)</h3>

<p>Instapaper also supports batch exports. On the web platform, navigate to any folder or tag, click the "Settings" icon in the folder header, and choose <strong>"Download as EPUB"</strong>. Instapaper packages all articles in that folder into a single multi-article EPUB, complete with a table of contents listing each saved piece.</p>

<p>This is ideal for long car journeys, flights, or any situation where you want a curated reading bundle ready on your e-reader. The folder-based export respects your organizational structure — create folders like "Read This Week" or "Travel Reading" and export them on demand.</p>

<h3>Exporting Your Highlights</h3>

<p>A lesser-known feature: Instapaper lets you export <strong>only your highlighted passages</strong> as EPUB. In the Highlights view, click "Export" and select EPUB format. The resulting file contains every passage you've highlighted across all your saved articles, each with a link back to the original article. It's essentially a personal research digest in ebook form.</p>

<h2 id="instapaper-10-features">What Instapaper 10 Means for EPUB Readers</h2>

<p>The Instapaper 10 update, released July 29, 2026, introduces several features that improve the EPUB reading ecosystem:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Why It Matters for EPUB Users</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Liquid Glass redesign</td>
      <td>Cleaner typography means better-formatted EPUB exports. The text rendering engine has been overhauled, so exported EPUBs look even cleaner on e-reader screens.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Four new fonts (Literata, New York, Open Sans, Atkinson Hyperlegible)</td>
      <td>These fonts carry into the EPUB export, giving you more typographic variety on your e-reader. Atkinson Hyperlegible is especially valuable for readers with visual impairments.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Rebuilt web platform</td>
      <td>The web version of Instapaper received its second-ever full rebuild since 2008. Faster syncing and more reliable article downloads mean EPUB exports are less likely to have broken images or truncated content.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Improved background sync</td>
      <td>Articles sync faster in the background, so your saved reading list is always up to date before you export to EPUB.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Home Screen widget</td>
      <td>Quick access to your current article means you can resume reading on your phone, then export to EPUB for deeper reading on your e-reader later.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="calibre-instapaper-workflow">Method 2: Calibre Automation for Batch EPUB Conversion</h2>

<p>If you have a large Instapaper library — hundreds or thousands of saved articles — the manual export workflow becomes tedious. This is where <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a> shines as an automation hub.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Download Your Complete Instapaper Archive</h3>

<p>Instapaper offers a full account export:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Log in to <strong>instapaper.com</strong> on the web.</li>
  <li>Go to <strong>Settings → Export</strong>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>"Download .zip file"</strong> — Instapaper generates a ZIP containing every article you've ever saved, each as a separate HTML file.</li>
</ol>

<p>This ZIP file is your raw archive. It contains the original HTML of every saved article, not the formatted version, so the quality depends on the source sites.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Import into Calibre</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Extract the ZIP to a folder on your computer.</li>
  <li>Open <a href="/blog/calibre-9-10-upgrade-2026/">Calibre (9.10 or later)</a>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Add Books → Add books from directories and sub-directories</strong>.</li>
  <li>Point Calibre to the extracted folder — it imports all HTML files as individual book entries.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 3: Batch Convert to EPUB</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Select all imported entries (Ctrl+A).</li>
  <li>Right-click → <strong>Convert → Bulk Convert</strong>.</li>
  <li>Set <strong>Output format: EPUB</strong>.</li>
  <li>Under <strong>EPUB Output</strong>, check "Insert metadata as page-break before text" for clean chapter breaks.</li>
  <li>Click OK. Calibre processes each article, converting the HTML to properly structured EPUB files with metadata, a table of contents, and consistent formatting.</li>
</ol>

<p>The result: your entire Instapaper archive transformed into a Calibre-managed EPUB library, ready to be side-loaded onto any e-reader or synced via Calibre's content server.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Transfer to E-Reader</h3>

<p>Calibre makes the final transfer easy. Connect your Kindle or Kobo via USB, select the EPUB files you want, and click <strong>Send to Device</strong>. Calibre automatically converts to the right format if needed — for example, converting EPUB to AZW3 for older Kindles, or to EPUB with Kobo-specific CSS fixes.</p>

<p>For wireless transfer, you can also use <a href="/blog/calibre-content-server-self-host-2026/">Calibre's Content Server</a> — a self-hosted web server that lets you browse and download your EPUB library from any browser on your local network, including your e-reader's built-in browser.</p>

<h2 id="browser-extension-method">Method 3: Browser Extensions for One-Click EPUB Downloads</h2>

<p>Several browser extensions streamline the Instapaper-to-EPUB workflow directly from your desktop browser, bypassing Instapaper's web interface entirely:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Tool</th>
      <th>How It Works</th>
      <th>Best For</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Dotepub</strong></td>
      <td>Bookmarklet that converts any open webpage to EPUB with one click. Works alongside Instapaper — save to Instapaper first, then export from the Instapaper reading view via Dotepub.</td>
      <td>Single-article conversion without leaving the browser</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Print Friendly & PDF</strong></td>
      <td>Cleans up web pages and exports to PDF or EPUB. The EPUB output preserves the article text and images, similar to Instapaper's built-in extraction.</td>
      <td>Articles that Instapaper's parser handles poorly (rare, but it happens with complex layouts)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Markdownload</strong></td>
      <td>Converts any webpage to Markdown, which can then be passed to <a href="/blog/markitdown-vs-pandoc-2026/">Pandoc or Markitdown</a> for EPUB conversion. Gives you full control over the final formatting.</td>
      <td>Power users who want to customize EPUB output</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Among these, <strong>Dotepub</strong> is the most Instapaper-friendly because it handles the article-to-EPUB conversion in one step without needing any intermediate format.</p>

<h2 id="epub-on-ereader-comparison">Best E-Readers for Instapaper EPUB Exports in 2026</h2>

<p>Once you have your EPUB files, any modern e-reader can display them. But some devices handle side-loaded EPUBs better than others:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>E-Reader</th>
      <th>EPUB Support</th>
      <th>Side-Loading Method</th>
      <th>Notes</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Kobo Libra Colour / Clara BW</strong></td>
      <td>Excellent native EPUB support</td>
      <td>USB, Dropbox, Calibre wireless, built-in browser</td>
      <td>Kobo reads standard EPUB without conversion. Supports KEPUB (Kobo's optimized format) via Calibre driver for faster page turns. Best choice for Instapaper exports.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>PocketBook Era Lite</strong></td>
      <td>Excellent (open ecosystem)</td>
      <td>USB, Dropbox, Calibre, Send-to-PocketBook email</td>
      <td>PocketBook reads EPUB natively, supports Adobe DRM, and has built-in text-to-speech. The Era Lite's E Ink Carta 1300 display offers the best contrast for article reading.</td>
    </tr>
