      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Instapaper doesn't have a native Kindle app, but you can use the Send to Kindle service — email your Instapaper EPUB exports to your Kindle email address (<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="3d4452484f535c50587d565453595158135e5250">[email&#160;protected]</a>), and Amazon converts them automatically. Since Amazon starting accepting EPUB as a source format in 2025, this workflow works without needing Calibre for the initial conversion.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">How do I export all my Instapaper articles at once?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Go to instapaper.com → Settings → Export → "Download .zip file." This downloads every article you've ever saved as HTML files. Import that ZIP into <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a> and batch-convert to EPUB. For folder-specific exports, navigate to any folder on the web and use the "Download as EPUB" option in the folder menu.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Do Instapaper EPUB exports include images and links?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Yes. Instapaper's EPUB export preserves embedded images, captions, and hyperlinks from the original article. Links remain clickable on e-readers that support hyperlinks (Kobo, PocketBook, Kindle with touch screen). Images are included at their original resolution, so loading times on older e-readers may be slower for image-heavy articles.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Is Instapaper free? Do I need a subscription for EPUB export?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Instapaper has a free tier that includes unlimited article saving, full-text search, and EPUB export. The Instapaper Premium subscription ($5.99/month or $39.99/year) adds full-text search across your entire archive, unlimited notes and highlights, and speed reading — but <strong>EPUB export is available on the free tier</strong>. You don't need to pay to get your articles onto your e-reader.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
